# People, ECO 2026 tasks 1 to 3: develop a common vision, manage conflicts, lead the project team.
# S(test, difficulty, skill, approach, stem, [key, distractors...], explanation)
# M(..., options with keys first, number of keys, explanation)  X(..., rows, columns, key per row, explanation)
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 2, "Manage conflicts", "A",
      "During a daily stand-up, two senior developers start arguing about which database design to use. The argument is getting personal and the rest of the team is uncomfortable. What should you do first?",
      ["Move the debate out of the stand-up, then meet both developers to understand and resolve it together",
       "Ask the solution architect to choose one design so the argument ends before it affects the sprint",
       "Postpone the topic until the next retrospective so that the current sprint is not disrupted by the argument",
       "Report the behavior to both developers' functional managers so they can address it as a conduct issue"],
      "Collaborating (problem solving) addresses the root of the conflict and keeps the relationship intact, and moving it out of the stand-up keeps that meeting short. Forcing a decision through the architect ends the argument but not the disagreement, delaying it to the retrospective is withdrawal, and escalating to managers comes only after the project manager has tried to resolve it."),
    S(1, 2, "Lead the project team", "H",
      "You are forming a hybrid team with members in three countries. In the first two weeks, meetings start late, people talk over each other and some members never speak. What is the best action?",
      ["Facilitate a session where the team agrees its own ground rules and records them in a team charter",
       "Publish a list of meeting rules yourself and remind members of them at the start of every meeting",
       "Ask human resources to schedule cultural awareness training for the team before the next phase",
       "Keep a log of who arrives late or interrupts others and share it with each member's manager at month end"],
      "Ground rules that the team writes itself in a team charter get far more buy-in than rules handed down. Training may help later but does not fix the immediate working norms, and logging behavior for managers damages trust."),
    S(1, 2, "Manage conflicts", "A",
      "Your team agreed that every code change needs a peer review before merging. One developer has merged changes without review three times this sprint. What should you do?",
      ["Speak privately with the developer about the agreed rule and the impact the skipped reviews are having",
       "Raise it at the next retrospective and ask the developer to explain the merges in front of the team",
       "Ask the tools administrator to remove the developer's permission to merge into the main branch",
       "Ask the developer's functional manager to add a note about the unreviewed merges to the next performance review"],
      "Managing a ground rule violation starts with a private, respectful conversation about the agreement and its impact. Public questioning embarrasses the person, and removing permissions or involving the functional manager are steps for when a direct conversation has failed."),
    S(1, 1, "Lead the project team", "A",
      "Your agile team is made up of experienced specialists who are used to organizing their own work. Which leadership approach best fits this team?",
      ["Servant leadership: remove impediments, shield the team from distractions and let members decide how to do the work",
       "Directive leadership: assign daily tasks to each specialist and check progress at the end of every day",
       "Transactional leadership: set individual targets and tie rewards closely to each person's output",
       "Laissez-faire leadership: stay out of the team's work entirely, including its impediments and any stakeholder requests"],
      "A skilled, self-organizing team needs a servant leader who removes obstacles and protects its focus. Directive and transactional styles undercut self-organization, and laissez-faire abandons the team when it needs help with blockers and stakeholders."),
    S(1, 2, "Develop a common vision", "H",
      "Halfway through a project, you notice that team members describe the project's goal in noticeably different ways, and some are building features that no stakeholder asked for. What should you do first?",
      ["Find out where the understanding diverged, then revisit the vision with the team and key stakeholders",
       "Rewrite the scope statement in more detail and send it to every team member with a read receipt",
       "Ask each team member to reread the project charter and confirm by email that they understand it",
       "Ask the sponsor to send the whole team a message restating the project's goals and its expected business benefits"],
      "A misunderstanding of the vision should be broken down to its root cause, then the vision is revisited with the people who own it so it is shared, not just restated. Sending documents or messages repeats the words without checking or rebuilding understanding."),
    S(1, 1, "Lead the project team", "A",
      "During backlog refinement, the team asks you how to split a large user story into smaller ones. What is the best response?",
      ["Encourage the team to decide how to split it, and offer a few splitting techniques if they want them",
       "Split the story yourself after the meeting and add the smaller stories to the backlog for the team",
       "Ask the product owner to split the story on their own and assign the smaller stories to members",
       "Ask the agile coach to take over backlog refinement until the team has more experience splitting stories"],
      "Empowering the team means letting it own how the work is broken down, with the project manager supporting rather than deciding. The product owner collaborates on splitting but does not do it alone or assign work, and handing refinement to someone else removes the team's learning."),
    M(1, 2, "Lead the project team", "H",
      "A new team member works eight hours ahead of the rest of the team and says they feel left out of decisions. Which TWO actions should you take? (Choose two.)",
      ["Rotate recurring meeting times so the inconvenience of early or late calls is shared",
       "Pair the new member with an experienced teammate who can act as a buddy",
       "Excuse the member from team meetings and send them the meeting notes instead",
       "Ask the member to work the main office's hours so they can join every discussion",
       "Assign the member only independent tasks that need little collaboration"],
      2,
      "Sharing the burden of meeting times and giving the person a buddy both build inclusion and connection. Sending notes instead of including them, demanding they work night hours, or isolating their work all make the feeling of exclusion worse."),
    S(1, 2, "Lead the project team", "H",
      "On your hybrid project, a vendor test data set was not delivered because two team members each believed the other was responsible for it. What is the best way to prevent this from happening again?",
      ["Work with the team to build a responsibility assignment matrix for the remaining deliverables",
       "Ask both team members to write an explanation of the missed deliverable for the project file",
       "Assign every remaining deliverable to yourself so that there is one point of accountability",
       "Add a weekly status meeting where each team member reads out everything they worked on during that week"],
      "A RACI or other responsibility assignment matrix makes each deliverable's owner explicit, which is exactly what was missing. Written explanations assign blame, taking every deliverable yourself does not scale, and longer status meetings do not clarify ownership."),
    X(1, 2, "Manage conflicts", "P",
      "For each situation, select the conflict resolution technique being used.",
      ["Each side gives up part of what it wanted so the two can reach a deal",
       "The project manager stresses points of agreement and plays down the differences",
       "The project manager imposes a decision because a safety deadline cannot wait",
       "The parties examine the data together and reach a solution both fully support"],
      ["Collaborate", "Compromise", "Smooth", "Force"],
      [1, 2, 3, 0],
      "Compromise means both sides give something up, smoothing (accommodating) emphasizes agreement over differences, forcing imposes one view and is justified mainly in emergencies such as safety, and collaborating (problem solving) works through the data to a solution everyone supports."),
    S(1, 2, "Lead the project team", "A",
      "In retrospectives your team rarely raises problems, even though you can see several issues slowing them down. What should you do?",
      ["Build psychological safety, for example by changing the retrospective format and being open about your own mistakes",
       "Require each team member to bring at least two problems to every retrospective from now on",
       "Invite the sponsor to the next retrospective so that the team can see how much senior leadership values the event",
       "Cancel retrospectives for a while because they are not producing improvement actions"],
      "People raise problems when they feel safe doing so; varying the format (anonymous input, small groups) and modeling openness build that safety. Quotas feel like a test, a senior visitor usually makes people more guarded, and dropping retrospectives removes the improvement loop."),
    S(1, 2, "Lead the project team", "A",
      "Your team's velocity has fallen for three sprints. A team member tells you that production support tickets are taking up a growing share of their day. What should you do?",
      ["Make the support work visible and agree with the team and product owner how much capacity it should get",
       "Ask the team to work extra hours for the next two sprints so that the velocity returns to its earlier level",
       "Report the velocity decline to the sponsor and ask for an additional developer for the team",
       "Raise the velocity target for the next sprint so the team has a clear goal to work toward"],
      "Hidden work is an impediment; making it visible lets the product owner and team plan capacity honestly. Overtime is not sustainable, asking for more people skips understanding the cause, and velocity is a planning measure, not a target to push."),
    S(1, 1, "Lead the project team", "P",
      "A newly formed team is arguing about how the work should be done, and members are testing your authority and each other's. Which stage of team development is the team in?",
      ["Storming", "Forming", "Norming", "Adjourning"],
      "In the Tuckman model, storming is when members push back, compete and disagree about approach after the polite forming stage. Norming comes when they settle on how to work together, and adjourning is the team's close."),
    S(1, 2, "Lead the project team", "A",
      "The sponsor has started going directly to developers in the middle of a sprint to ask for small new features. The developers feel they cannot refuse. What should you do?",
      ["Explain to the sponsor how new requests reach the team through the product owner and the backlog",
       "Tell the developers to accept the sponsor's requests, since the sponsor is funding the project",
       "Ask the developers to log the hours spent on these requests so the cost can be reported later",
       "Ask the product owner to cancel the sprint and start a new one that includes the sponsor's requests"],
      "Part of leading an agile team is representing the team and protecting the sprint; requests go to the product owner, who prioritizes them in the backlog. Accepting side requests undermines the sprint goal, tracking hours does not fix the behavior, and cancelling a sprint is an extreme step reserved for an obsolete sprint goal."),
    S(1, 2, "Lead the project team", "H",
      "In design sessions, a senior engineer quickly dismisses ideas from junior team members, and the juniors have stopped contributing. What should you do?",
      ["Use a facilitation technique such as silent brainstorming so every idea is captured before discussion",
       "Ask the senior engineer to stop attending design sessions so that junior members feel free to speak",
       "Let the team sort it out, since self-organizing teams are expected to resolve their own dynamics",
       "Have the junior members focus on building the designs and leave design decisions to the senior engineer"],
      "Structured facilitation gives every voice a fair hearing and makes use of the team's varied perspectives. Removing the senior engineer loses expertise, leaving it alone lets the pattern continue, and sidelining juniors wastes their ideas."),
    S(1, 3, "Lead the project team", "H",
      "Some team members have started using a generative AI tool to draft test cases. Others worry about accuracy and about company data being entered into the tool. What is the best action?",
      ["Facilitate the team in agreeing ground rules for AI use, including human review and data handling, within company policy",
       "Ban the tool on the project until the organization has finished evaluating generative AI products",
       "Allow unrestricted use of the tool, since it shortens test preparation and the team can always fix any errors it finds later",
       "Let each team member decide individually whether and how to use the tool for their own work"],
      "Agreeing team norms for AI, such as review of outputs and what data may be shared, within organizational policy, captures the benefit while addressing both concerns. A blanket ban gives up the benefit, unrestricted use ignores real risks, and individual choices leave the team without shared rules."),

    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 1, "Manage conflicts", "A",
      "During a sprint review, a developer and a quality assurance analyst begin openly criticizing each other's work in front of stakeholders. What should you do first?",
      ["Pause the discussion and meet with both of them privately afterward to understand the disagreement",
       "Tell the two of them in the meeting that the quality analyst's view is final on quality matters",
       "Continue the meeting and let them finish the argument, since the stakeholders may want to hear both sides",
       "Report the incident to the quality manager and ask for the analyst to be replaced on the project"],
      "A conflict is best handled in private and by first understanding its source. Ruling in public is forcing, letting an argument play out before stakeholders harms the team, and replacing someone before understanding the issue is premature."),
    S(2, 2, "Manage conflicts", "H",
      "Integration testing keeps failing, and the hardware team and the software team blame each other in every meeting. What is the best approach?",
      ["Bring both teams together to analyze the failures and agree on a shared way of working at the interface",
       "Assign one team the responsibility for all integration defects so accountability is clear from now on",
       "Ask each team to document its own view of the failures and send the documents to the sponsor",
       "Hold separate meetings for each team so that they no longer meet until the integration phase is finished"],
      "The conflict comes from a shared interface, so a joint analysis focused on the problem rather than on people resolves it. Assigning blame, escalating competing documents or keeping the teams apart all leave the cause in place."),
    S(2, 2, "Lead the project team", "P",
      "Your new team is made up mostly of junior staff who have not used the project's tools before. Which leadership approach is most appropriate at the start?",
      ["Give clear direction and hands-on coaching, then delegate more as the team builds skill",
       "Delegate all decisions to the team from day one so that members learn by solving problems",
       "Keep a hands-off role and step in only when a deadline is about to be missed",
       "Focus on rewards and penalties tied to each person's weekly output to keep productivity high"],
      "Situational leadership matches style to the team's readiness: more direction and coaching for an inexperienced team, more delegation as competence grows. Full delegation or a hands-off approach sets a junior team up to struggle, and pure reward-and-penalty management does not build skill."),
    S(2, 1, "Lead the project team", "A",
      "A newly formed agile team is about to start its first sprint. Which should the team establish first?",
      ["Working agreements and a shared definition of done",
       "Individual velocity targets for each team member",
       "A detailed task schedule covering the next six sprints",
       "A list of team members ranked by seniority"],
      "Working agreements and a definition of done set the expectations a team needs to work together and judge when work is finished. Velocity is a team measure, not an individual target, detailed six-sprint task schedules contradict iterative planning, and seniority rankings do not help a self-organizing team."),
    S(2, 2, "Lead the project team", "A",
      "One of the strongest developers on your agile team has become quiet and disengaged. In conversation, they mention they feel they are not learning anything new. What should you do?",
      ["Explore the member's development goals and look for project work that builds the skills they want",
       "Ask the functional manager to approve a one-time bonus to recognize the member's past performance",
       "Take no action for now, because the member's deliverables are still on time and of good quality",
       "Mention the disengagement to the functional manager so it is noted before the annual review"],
      "Motivation that comes from growth is addressed by connecting the person to meaningful development, which a project manager can often do through assignments. A bonus does not answer the stated need, ignoring it risks losing the person, and reporting it as a problem damages trust."),
    S(2, 3, "Develop a common vision", "H",
      "On a hybrid project, the product owner and the sponsor describe the minimum viable product differently, and the team is getting conflicting direction. What should you do?",
      ["Facilitate a session with the sponsor and product owner to agree the product vision and the scope of the MVP",
       "Follow the product owner's version, because the product owner is the person accountable for the product backlog",
       "Follow the sponsor's version, because the sponsor is accountable for the project's funding and benefits",
       "Ask the team to build the features from both versions so the conflict can be settled at the demo"],
      "The root cause is a vision that is not shared by two key stakeholders; the project manager facilitates alignment between them. Picking either person's version leaves the other misaligned and the conflict will return, and building both wastes capacity."),
    S(2, 2, "Manage conflicts", "A",
      "At a retrospective, several team members say the working agreements they wrote at the start are no longer being followed and some no longer make sense. What should happen next?",
      ["The team reviews its working agreements together and updates them",
       "You rewrite the working agreements and present the new version",
       "The product owner decides which of the agreements stay in force",
       "The team drops the agreements and relies on everyday common sense"],
      "Working agreements belong to the team and should evolve; a retrospective is the right place for the team to revise them. Agreements written by the project manager or product owner lose ownership, and dropping them removes the shared norms."),
    M(2, 2, "Lead the project team", "H",
      "You are leading a newly formed team whose members have never met in person. Which TWO actions will most help build trust? (Choose two.)",
      ["Hold a video kickoff where members share their backgrounds and working styles",
       "Agree communication norms such as expected response times and core hours",
       "Monitor each person's online status to confirm they are working their hours",
       "Communicate only by email so there is always a written record of decisions",
       "Divide the work so that members rarely need to depend on one another"],
      2,
      "Getting to know each other and agreeing how to communicate are foundations of trust in a virtual team. Monitoring online status signals distrust, email-only communication is slow and impersonal, and avoiding interdependence prevents trust from ever being built."),
    S(2, 1, "Lead the project team", "P",
      "A normally calm team member has become short-tempered with colleagues since the company announced a reorganization. What should you do first?",
      ["Check in privately with the team member and listen to their concerns",
       "Remind the whole team about the code of conduct at the next team meeting",
       "Ask human resources to speak with the member about the behavior",
       "Reassign the member's tasks until the reorganization is complete"],
      "Emotional intelligence starts with noticing a change and listening. A team-wide reminder does not reach the cause, involving human resources first escalates too quickly, and removing work may add to the person's worry."),
    S(2, 2, "Lead the project team", "A",
      "Your team has spent two meetings debating which test automation tool to adopt and needs a decision by the end of the week. What is the best way to move forward?",
      ["Use a quick group decision technique such as fist of five, then discuss the objections it reveals",
       "Choose the tool yourself, since the team has already spent too long on the decision",
       "Ask the sponsor to pick the tool, since it will be paid for from the project budget",
       "Postpone the choice until a later sprint, when the team will have more time to evaluate every candidate tool"],
      "Fist of five and similar techniques show the level of support quickly and surface the specific concerns to resolve, keeping the decision with the team. Deciding for the team or handing it to the sponsor removes ownership, and postponing ignores the deadline."),
    X(2, 2, "Lead the project team", "H",
      "For each behavior, select the leadership style it shows.",
      ["The leader clears blockers and protects the team's focus so members can deliver",
       "The leader decides alone and then tells the team what will happen",
       "The leader sets no direction and leaves the team to set its own goals",
       "The leader asks questions that help a member find their own solution"],
      ["Servant", "Directive", "Laissez-faire", "Coaching"],
      [0, 1, 2, 3],
      "Servant leaders remove impediments and serve the team, directive leaders decide and instruct, laissez-faire leaders stay hands-off, and coaching leaders develop people by guiding them to their own answers."),
    S(2, 3, "Lead the project team", "H",
      "Customers keep finding defects after each release. The team says the test environment is to blame, while the testers say requirements are unclear. What should you do first?",
      ["Lead the team through a root cause analysis of the escaped defects before choosing a fix",
       "Buy a new test environment, since the team has identified it as the source of the problem",
       "Ask the business analyst to rewrite every requirement in more detail before the next release",
       "Add a second round of manual testing at the end of every release to catch the defects"],
      "With competing explanations, the next step is a structured root cause analysis using the defect data. Acting on one group's theory, whether new environments or rewritten requirements, may spend money on the wrong cause, and more end-of-line testing treats the symptom."),
    S(2, 2, "Lead the project team", "H",
      "A team member who is deaf has joined your team. Most team communication happens in voice calls. What should you do?",
      ["Ask the team member which accommodations work best for them and adjust meeting practices to match",
       "Move all team communication to email so that every member receives information in the same way",
       "Ask a colleague to summarize each voice call for the new member in a short chat message after it ends",
       "Assign the member work that needs the least discussion so meetings are less of a barrier"],
      "Supporting varied needs starts with asking the person, then adapting practices (for example live captions, chat alongside calls). Email-only slows the whole team, after-the-fact summaries exclude the person from discussion, and limiting their work is discriminatory."),
    S(2, 1, "Lead the project team", "A",
      "In the daily stand-up, team members face you and report their progress to you rather than talking to each other. What should you do?",
      ["Coach the team that the stand-up is for coordinating with each other, and step back from leading it",
       "Keep running the stand-up, since team members are giving complete and useful status updates",
       "Replace the stand-up with a written status report that each team member sends to you every morning instead",
       "Ask the product owner to run the stand-up instead so that updates focus on business priorities"],
      "The stand-up is the team's planning and coordination event, not a status report to a manager. A servant leader coaches the team to own it. Keeping it as reporting, replacing it with written status or handing it to the product owner all miss its purpose."),
    S(2, 2, "Manage conflicts", "H",
      "Read the case study. The installation contractor's field lead and the app team lead disagree about the data format the new meters will send. Each says the other side must change. What should you do?",
      ["Bring both leads together to review what each side needs and agree an interface both can support",
       "Tell the app team to adapt to the contractor's format, because the fixed-price contract is already signed",
       "Tell the contractor to adapt to the app team's format, because the app delivers the business case",
       "Raise the disagreement with the steering committee and ask it to choose one of the two formats"],
      "This is a conflict over a shared interface, so the project manager first brings the parties together to solve the problem collaboratively. Imposing either side's format is forcing without analysis, and the steering committee is an escalation path for when the project cannot resolve it.",
      stim="PMP-CS2", step=1),

    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 1, "Manage conflicts", "P",
      "Two team members have had several sharp exchanges this week. You are not yet sure what the disagreement is about. What should you do first?",
      ["Talk with each of them to identify the source of the conflict",
       "Separate them by assigning them to different work packages",
       "Tell them both that the next incident will be escalated to HR",
       "Ask the team to vote on which member's position is correct"],
      "Conflict management starts with identifying its source and context. Separating people, issuing warnings or putting it to a vote all act before anyone knows what the conflict is about."),
    S(0, 1, "Lead the project team", "P",
      "Your new team has just come together. Members are polite but unsure of their roles and of what the project is trying to achieve. What should you focus on?",
      ["Clarifying the project's goals and each member's role, and helping members get to know each other",
       "Letting members work out their own roles, since the team will settle into a pattern on its own",
       "Setting strict performance targets so that members understand the standard expected of them",
       "Assigning the hardest tasks first so that you can quickly identify the strongest performers on the team"],
      "A team in the forming stage needs direction: clear goals, clear roles and a chance to build relationships. Leaving it to chance, setting strict targets or testing people with hard tasks does not address its uncertainty."),
    S(0, 1, "Lead the project team", "A",
      "At sprint planning, the team asks you to assign the sprint's tasks to each person, as their previous manager did. What should you do?",
      ["Encourage team members to choose their own tasks and coach them through the first few plans",
       "Assign the tasks this time and plan to hand the job over once the team is more experienced",
       "Ask the product owner to assign the tasks, since the product owner knows the priorities best",
       "Let the most senior developer assign the tasks so that the work is matched to members' skills"],
      "Self-organizing teams decide among themselves who does what; the project manager coaches them into that habit. Assigning tasks, whether by the project manager, the product owner or a senior developer, keeps the old dependency in place."),
    M(0, 2, "Lead the project team", "A",
      "Which TWO behaviors are signs of a high-performing team? (Choose two.)",
      ["Members hold each other accountable for agreed commitments",
       "Members raise concerns openly and early, even when they are uncomfortable",
       "The project manager approves every technical decision before work begins",
       "Members avoid disagreeing with each other to keep the atmosphere positive",
       "Work is always assigned according to each member's seniority"],
      2,
      "High-performing teams share accountability and are safe enough to raise problems early. Central approval of every decision, avoiding healthy conflict and seniority-based assignment are signs of a team that is not yet performing."),
]
